// ── Templates added 2026-09-25 — the rest of the contractor book ────────────
//
// The owner: "those services … should have a few line items as part of the
// service" — the quote builder now adds a service WITH its template lines,
// so a row with no template opens as one bare line. Every commonly quoted row
// above now carries 2–5 lines: the labour, the materials the job buys and the
// fee it carries (permit, bin, disposal, service call).
//
// Evidence, row by row where it exists:
//   • the captured general-contractor and construction template sets
//     (2026-09-24): deck footings and framing $28 a sq ft, railing $35 a
//     linear ft, patio hardscape $14 a sq ft, window replacement labour $200
//     a window, builder-grade kitchen install $3,500, bath install $4,500 —
//     prices kept, costs ours (labour ≈ 50 %, material ≈ 75 %);
//   • the captured handyman set: service call $89, deck board $75, fence
//     section $175;
//   • roofing_service.js's own figures (tear-off $85 a square, shingle
//     labour $175, ridge $5.50 a linear ft, seamless gutters ≈ $10 installed),
//     so a GC's roofing line never undercuts the roofer's seed;
//   • the Home Depot reference (_materialCosts.js) wherever the material is
//     one it carries; a material it does not carry (ready-mix, rebar, pavers,
//     membranes, equipment) is a plain line at a 2026 supplier price.
//
// Measured lines carry the key the takeoff that measures them produces —
// `areaSqFt` for a typed or reuse-takeoff area (floor, tiled, slab), `areaSqft`
// for a traced paving outline, `wallSqft` for paint and wallcovering,
// `linearFt` for a room's perimeter (trim), `perimeterLf` / `gateCount` /
// `fencePosts` for a fence run, `concreteCuYd` for a pour, the roof report's
// `squares` / `eaveFt` / `perimeterFt` / `ridgeFt` / `gutterFt` / `downspouts`
// for roof-edge work, `doorCount` / `drawerCount` for cabinets and `each` for
// a count typed on the estimate. A flat row whose lines are measured has no
// flat preset (rangeBasis "measured"): inventing a typical area would be
// padding absent data. Rows the rate cards price structurally (`existing`)
// keep their flat template as the GC's all-in starting point.
//
// Quote types without a seed file of their own that sell a row are named in
// its `categories`, the file's own trade first (lib/services/confirmServices.js
// NEAREST_TRADES): concrete, masonry, paving, excavation, demolition,
// demolition_contractor, junk_removal, insulation, restoration, epoxy,
// tiling, countertop, remodeling, doors_windows and glass. Seeded trades
// (roofing, plumbing, HVAC, electrical, flooring, fencing) are never tagged:
// their own files already carry the canonical row.
//
// New lines carry Punjabi inline (t8): a per-line pair cannot collide with
// another template's line of the same English name in the i18n lines map.
// The service names are already on each row from the i18n file; T() still
// asks for them, so N() reads them from there rather than re-typing them.
const t8 = (en, fr, es, it, de, uk, pa, tl) => ({ en, fr, es, it, de, uk, pa, tl });
const N = (key) => {
  const e = I18N.services[key];
  return { it: e.it, de: e.de, uk: e.uk, pa: e.pa, tl: e.tl };
};
const GC = "general_contracting";
const tag = (...types) => ({ categories: [GC, ...types] });
const k = (key) => `fq.general_contracting.${key}`;

